on-arrival: {!
Q.orlen_prices_2023_done = 1;
Q.state_company_politicisation =
    (Number(Q.state_company_politicisation) || 0) + 12;
Q.public_mood_pending_social_spending_support += 5;
Q.public_mood_pending_social_spending_salience += 6;
Q.news_headline =
    "Fuel falls below six złoty five weeks before polling day";
!}

= September 2023 — A price that is not a market

Fuel at the state-controlled refiner drops below six złoty a litre in the weeks
before the election, well under regional prices and under what the company's own
margins can sustain. Management calls it commercial policy. Drivers call it
cheap. By late September some stations are rationing, and tankers are arriving
late to independent operators who cannot buy at the same price.

The mechanism matters more than the price. A company in which the Treasury
holds the decisive stake has been used to move a household cost inside an
election period, and the same instrument will still exist whoever wins.

[? if left_in_government = 1: Our own ministers sit in a cabinet with Treasury
supervision duties, so any complaint we make has to survive being asked what we
did about it.?][? if left_in_government = 0: In opposition we can describe the
mechanism, demand documents and prepare the rule; we cannot instruct a single
board.?]

- @orlen_governance
- @orlen_price_populist
- @orlen_shortage
- @orlen_audit

@orlen_governance
title: Attack the mechanism and demand a state-company governance rule
subtitle: Makes political pricing a permanent institutional argument rather than a five-week complaint.
on-arrival: state_company_governance_pressure += 10; institutional_trust += 4; administrative_reform_pressure += 4; public_trust += 2; pis_relation -= 5; media_capacity += 2; institutional_issue_salience += 3; news_headline = "The Left demands a rule against election pricing in state companies"

We publish the supervisory chain: who appoints the board, who approves the
margin, who signs the ministerial recommendation, and which of them can be
required to answer in writing. The demand is a standing rule — political
pricing declared, costed and reported — not an accusation about one autumn.

It is a dull argument during a campaign about who loves Poland more. It is
also the only version of this fight that still exists after the votes are
counted, when some other cabinet inherits the same instrument.

- @poland_event_queue: File the governance demand

@orlen_price_populist
title: Say only that fuel should be cheap and that the price will rise after the vote
subtitle: The most repeatable line available; concedes that state companies may be priced politically, provided somebody else is doing it.
on-arrival: public_trust += 1; left_poll_momentum += 0.2; workers_support += 2; rural_support += 2; institutional_trust -= 3; state_company_politicisation += 4; progressives_dissent += 3; news_headline = "The opposition promises the price rise arrives the week after the election"

The line is short, true and lands everywhere: enjoy it now, because the bill
comes in November. Rural and commuter audiences who ignore governance
arguments repeat this one without prompting.

We have also told the country that the real complaint is the timing rather
than the instrument. Nobody in the next cabinet will forget that the Left
found election pricing objectionable mainly because it was not ours.

- @poland_event_queue: Take the price line into the campaign

@orlen_shortage
title: Take evidence from station managers and independent hauliers
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Converts the price stunt into a documented supply failure with named businesses on the record.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: No research or legal team is free to collect supply evidence.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; state_company_governance_pressure += 6; administrative_capacity += 1 if left_in_government = 1; institutional_trust += 3; public_trust += 3; media_capacity += 3; market_liberal_support += 3; pis_relation -= 6; news_headline = "Empty pumps and late tankers are documented station by station"

Independent station owners, hauliers and rural operators put delivery dates,
allocation letters and empty-tank photographs on the record. The story stops
being about whether cheap fuel is nice and becomes about a supply system bent
to a calendar.

The evidence is unglamorous and slow, and it is the material that any later
inquiry, audit or prosecution will actually need. It also gives small business
owners a reason to answer a Left canvasser's call.

- @poland_event_queue: Bank the supply evidence

@orlen_audit
title: Put the refinery's margin under the audit chamber
subtitle: Requires an open referral channel. Reaches the board papers rather than the pump price.
choose-if: nik_referral_channel = 1
unavailable-subtitle: We have no channel into the Supreme Audit Office, and a state company answers parliamentary questions with a press release.
