= December 2023 — The chamber outdraws the evening news

The parliamentary channel's live stream is being watched by hundreds of
thousands of people at a time, clipped, captioned and shared by accounts that
have never covered politics. Committee sittings run past midnight to an
audience that stays. Journalists call it Sejmflix; the deputies discover that
their questions are now content, and that a well-prepared eleven-minute
committee exchange travels further than a month of press releases.

Attention of this kind does not last and cannot be commanded. It can be spent
on substance, on personalities, or on nothing at all.

[? if left_in_government = 1: Our ministers are now performing at the
despatch box in front of an audience that did not choose to watch a
minister.?][? if left_in_government = 0: From the opposition benches this is
the cheapest media access the party has had in years, and the only one nobody
can withdraw.?]
[? if left_dominant_current = "spring": Wiosna's people have been waiting for
exactly this format since 2019.?][? if left_dominant_current = "razem":
Razem's deputies want the committee work broadcast, not the theatre.?]
[? if media_capacity >= 35: Our own media operation is already large enough
that we could feed this audience directly rather than through the chamber's
own feed.?]

Whatever we choose, one thing changes for everybody: a parliament that is
being watched continuously is a parliament in which a microphone left open is
no longer a private embarrassment.

- @sejmflix_substance
- @sejmflix_personalities
- @sejmflix_ignore

@sejmflix_substance
title: Put committee work and legislative detail in front of the new audience
subtitle: Converts attention into durable political literacy; slower and less shareable than the alternative.
on-arrival: parliament_visibility += 4; media_capacity += 3; public_trust += 3; institutional_trust += 3; institutional_issue_salience += 3; left_poll_momentum += 0.1; organiser_energy -= 2; news_headline = "The Left sends its committee work to the new parliamentary audience"

Our deputies prepare for committees the way they would prepare for a
television studio: documents in advance, one argument per sitting, the
question written so that the answer is the story. Housing, labour inspection
and hospital contracting get hours of national attention they would never have
bought.

The clips are less viral than a shouting match, and the work of producing them
falls on a small number of exhausted people. What it builds is an audience
that understands a legislative stage — which is worth more in the third year
than in the first.

- @poland_event_queue: Take the committee strategy to the desk

