= December 2024 — The loop closes

The subsidy decision is appealed, and the appeal belongs by statute to the
Supreme Court chamber that handles electoral and public affairs — the chamber
staffed through the disputed appointment process, whose rulings this
government has spent a year declining to treat as fully valid.

The trap is symmetrical and complete. If the chamber restores the subsidy, a
body the coalition calls unlawful has just overruled the electoral commission
and paid the opposition tens of millions. If the coalition refuses to execute
that ruling, it is a government declining to obey a court over money owed to
its main rival. Either way, somebody's legitimacy argument is being used to
move public funds.

[? if left_in_government = 1: Our ministers will have to sign or refuse the
transfer, and no version of that signature is clean.?][? if
left_in_government = 0: We can name the trap without having to walk into it,
which is worth saying honestly rather than pretending it is a strategy.?]

- @funding_chamber_legislate
- @funding_chamber_comply
- @funding_chamber_refuse

@funding_chamber_legislate
title: Move party-finance appeals to an undisputed court by statute
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Fixes the jurisdiction rather than the case; the pending appeal is unaffected and the President may veto.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: No legislative capacity is free to redraft the electoral appeal route.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; judicial_legitimacy += 5; institutional_trust += 5; constitutional_restraint += 4; administrative_reform_pressure += 3; public_trust += 3; president_relation -= 3; news_headline = "A bill moves party-finance appeals out of the disputed chamber"

The bill does one thing: electoral and party-finance appeals go to a chamber
whose composition nobody disputes. It does not touch the pending case, because
legislation aimed at a live appeal is the disease rather than the cure.

The Palace may well veto it, and the current appeal will still be decided
where it was filed. What changes is that the next one is not another test of
whether the state recognises its own courts.

- @poland_event_queue: Table the jurisdiction bill

@funding_chamber_comply
title: Commit in advance to executing the ruling whichever way it goes
subtitle: Buys back rule-of-law credibility at the cost of funding a rival and infuriating our own voters.
on-arrival: constitutional_restraint += 8; institutional_trust += 6; judicial_legitimacy += 3; eu_relations += 3; public_trust += 1; left_poll_momentum -= 0.2; progressives_dissent += 4; pis_finance_pressure -= 8; news_headline = "The coalition promises to execute the subsidy ruling in advance"

We say before the ruling that it will be executed. That is the only sentence
that distinguishes a government repairing the rule of law from one relitigating
it, and saying it in advance is the only version that counts.

Our own supporters do not want to hear that a disputed chamber may be about to
hand tens of millions to PiS and that we will pay it. They hear it anyway, and
some of them will not come back for it.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the advance commitment

@funding_chamber_refuse
title: Treat any ruling from that chamber as legally non-existent
subtitle: Consistent with the coalition's own legitimacy argument and it converts a finance dispute into a constitutional one.
on-arrival: supreme_court_chamber_recognition -= 10; constitutional_restraint -= 8; institutional_trust -= 6; eu_relations -= 4; judicial_legitimacy -= 5; pis_finance_pressure += 10; left_poll_momentum += 0.1; news_headline = "The disputed chamber's subsidy ruling is declared a legal nullity"

The position is at least coherent: a body improperly constituted cannot issue
a valid ruling, and that has been the coalition's argument since before it
took office. The subsidy stays cut and the opposition's finances stay broken.

It also means that the state now has two answers to the question of which
courts exist, and that the answer depends on who benefits. Every institution
watching this — the Commission, the audit chamber, our own prosecutors —
adjusts its expectations accordingly.

- @poland_event_queue: Declare the ruling a nullity


